<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frankenstein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Frankenstein; or, The Modern Prometheus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frankenstein; or, The Modern Prometheus is a Gothic novel by the English author <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-shelley">Mary Shelley</a>, published anonymously in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 1 January 1818. Its protagonist, the young scientist Victor Frankenstein, creates a living creature from body parts and is horrified by what he has made. Shelley, aged 18, conceived it by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake Geneva</a> in the cold summer of 1816, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a> proposed that he and his companions each write a ghost story. It spawned a genre of horror stories, films and plays and is generally considered one of the first science fiction novels.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frankenstein.jpg" alt="Frankenstein" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Theodor von Holst · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frontispiece_to_Frankenstein_1831.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
